---
description: How to fan out work — subagent spawning, model tiers, scale-for-correctness
---

- One focused pass when one context holds the task; fan out the moment it outgrows one —
  a sweep too large for one context, an audit needing independent lenses, a many-file
  migration, an adversarial verify. The most exhaustive correct answer is the goal.
- Orchestrate on the strong model; execute on the smallest doer model the harness offers
  (Cursor → composer-2.5, Claude Code → haiku). The orchestrator never writes code itself.
- Spawn DYNAMIC subagents — an inline task, not a predefined agent type.
- Inject into every spawn: project + phase, "read the Kavach DB don't re-derive",
  research-first (skill + current sources, never weights), and a RETURN CONTRACT
  (a writer persists its decision to Kavach the same turn; a read-only agent returns a
  structured result for the orchestrator to persist). A finding not written is lost.
- A delegated result is inference until its landed artifact proves it.
